  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through next Wednesday...\n\nAs the region remains under persistent northwest flow, strong low\nto mid levels winds will exit the region to the southeast. Wind\ngusts 30-45 mph can be expected to decrease through this \nafternoon. As weak energy continues to move over the region under \nnorthwest flow, isolated showers and a few thunderstorms are \nexpected this afternoon and again Thursday afternoon when more \nscattered showers are expected. Into Saturday, ensembles show the \nmid level pattern flattening with a shortwave moving through. The \nenergy and best forcing looks to split to the north and south, so \nthe confidence for stronger thunderstorms is low at this time, but\nthat can change. Atmospheric moisture looks to increase into the \nweekend as southerly low level flow pulls in moisture from the \nGulf. Even if thunderstorm potential is low, beneficial\nprecipitation could be seen, especially across southeast MT. \nCurrently there is about a 40-60% chance for at least half an inch\nof precipitation over the weekend across the southeast. With \nensembles keeping the low to the north into Monday, low to \nmoderate precipitation chances remain in the forecast (highest in \nthe high terrain). By mid week clusters show a general ridging \npattern over the western US with a moderate chance for unsettled \nconditions. High temperatures through the period will remain \nseasonal, in the 70s to low 80s. TS",
